[{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"231228c010541561a81dcb1353ac200f5c791b1a","body":"[hermit2, recon-2, claude-opus-5, devbig014, role=impl]\n\nPlain Language Summary and Project Impact: three backend dispatch tests assert\nthe guest's exit status with a bare `assert_eq!` and no message, so a failure\nprints `left: Exited(255) right: Exited(0)` and nothing else. The guest's own\nstderr -- the only thing that says WHY the launch failed -- is sitting in the\nsame `output` struct, two fields away, and the very next assertions already read\nit. This adds it to the failure message. The tests still fail; they now say\nsomething.\n\nTask: four-named-assertions-across-four-backends-are-what-keeps-main-red\n\nWHY NOW. Both DBT dispatch tests are failing on main today with exactly that\noutput. I could establish from the retained logs that the guest exits 255 having\ncompleted 0 syscalls with an empty thread tree in 8ms, and could NOT establish\nwhy, because the explanation was discarded at the assertion. Whoever fixes DBT\nneeds that text, and today they would have to re-run to get it.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It weakens nothing: the same condition fails, on the same\nline, with strictly more information. No assertion is relaxed, no tolerance\nwidened, no case skipped.\n\nTHE THREE SITES are the LiteInst, DBT and KVM dispatch tests, which share the\nshape. The fourth backend test, dbt_public_status_dispatch_runs_true_through_\ndetcore, is deliberately NOT changed: it calls `run_with_backend` rather than\n`run_with_output_backend` and receives only a status, so there is no stderr to\nreport and adding a message would only restate the numbers.\n\nDeterminism: no guest-visible behaviour changes; this touches test failure\nmessages only.\n\nLinux Semantics: unchanged.\n\nValidation: `cargo check --locked -p hermit --lib` and `--all-targets` both\nfinish clean. `rustfmt --edition 2024 --check hermit-cli/src/lib.rs` returns 0 --\nnote the crate is edition 2024, and checking it with `--edition 2021` reports\npre-existing let-chain errors that have nothing to do with this change.\n","headRefName":"recon-2/dbt-failure-diagnostics","headRefOid":"612d06ab9d047ef0583e5e91d44a3a5e84d3c0f8","isDraft":false,"labels":[],"number":3058,"state":"OPEN","title":"Show the guest's stderr when a backend launch fails its status assertion","updatedAt":"2026-09-17T08:03:13Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3058"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"cdf828ddeb381a51e493b65c1f348f0dae2135e3","body":"[hermit2, degraded-unresolved, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nPreserve the random bytes consumed before SaBRe initializes Detcore, so the real root thread continues the same deterministic stream. The loader's authenticated initial random state and completed AT_RANDOM initialization now transfer into the normal Detcore thread; missing proof cannot create a replacement stream. The installer also stages the corrected Cargo-built SaBRe loader, and the landed DBT dependency fixes GNU TLS generation for on-demand clients. These changes address concrete failures on Hermit's path to faithful deterministic Linux execution.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe owned supervisor authenticates the actual image, process generation, configuration, held object bytes and loader phase. Initial transfer, kernel-observed later exec continuation, and supported initial static dispatch use distinct typed results. Ordinary wrapper frames and kernel SIGILL/UD0 stops retain separate proofs. Raw Unix paths and kernel comm bytes are handled without decoding unrelated opaque bytes as UTF-8. Larger original-image snapshots use bounded copying into anonymous read-only file-backed storage; the 128 MiB threshold selects storage rather than rejecting the image. Cleanup stays with the supervisor's owned children, removing the redundant numeric-PID signal after reap.\n\nThe installer now copies the loader from reverie-sabre's bundled Cargo build rather than rebuilding the old SaBRe gitlink. It checks the actual source revision, preserves the one-line sabre.revision format, records the distinct upstream revision, and stages the corresponding SaBRe/libelf licenses. Official artifact paths and backend selectors remain unchanged.\n\nAll 21 active Cargo references and 26 lock source rows now select actually landed Reverie **6ae69f57fa9d675b503f5e631140644e652724ab**. The ten-commit range from b304 includes bootstrap/continuation (https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/564 and https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/566), mapping protections (https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/568), and GNU TLS client generation (https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/569). That lineage also contains KVM proc/fdinfo behavior, ordered guest-log facilities and lifecycle/RPC cleanup, including draining completed connection tasks and checked IDs. The final main base already selects596b and its entire KVM subtree equals retained6ae, so these facts are not presented as a newly introduced KVM change or runtime certification by this pin. Parent and nested gitlinks are unchanged.\n\nThe final DBT change adds GNU-only `-mtls-dialect=gnu` to the on-demand client producer, matching the installed producer and avoiding unsupported TLSDESC relocations. The full DBT subtree and client flags therefore differ. The DynamoRIO SDK key inputs remain equal; both ledgers explicitly preserve the existing 16-job and 1050-effective-job-second policy on that narrower evidence. No depth assertion or failure status is suppressed.\n\n## Determinism\n\nEarly random requests use the shared Detcore Pcg64Mcg implementation, including flag validation, draw consumption and partial-memory-fault behavior. Versioned image/configuration/generation-bound state is consumed exactly once into a virgin root. The transfer neither replaces ThreadState nor imports unrelated clocks or scheduler metadata. Completed initial auxv initialization is recorded once without drawing or emitting it again later. Exec continuation carries no RNG state and requires the owned EXEC event. Missing or stale evidence remains an error.\n\nThis does not establish complete early syscall/clock accounting or universal cross-backend parity. Existing strict comparisons, guest commands and runtime limits remain mandatory.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nObject proof retains held FD/device/inode, root-relative canonical/no-follow path, complete original bytes, exact frame/instruction identity and process generation. Original vDSO bytes are captured before loader rewriting; absence needs maps/auxv agreement. Corrected upstream mapping code restores original PROT bits rather than mixing mapping flags into permissions. The public libc getrandom detour forwards transparently; rewritten syscalls remain the entropy routing boundary, with actual callback forwarding controls covering both caller domains.\n\nRemaining limits are explicit: large snapshots require supported Btrfs/ext/XFS cache backing and available OS/storage/address resources; filesystem type does not prove physical disk backing. Repeated complete-object authentication has a cost. Collisions with the private prctl refuse fatally, and intervening signal/syscall events can invalidate saved SIGILL proof. Complete static/exec/shebang and Linux signal compatibility remain unproven.\n\n## Validation\n\nAt frozen **c961f85503dee4629c00981a7707afe39da71a3e**, the normal merge incorporates actually landed main cdf828d while preserving the reviewed consumer and actual6ae dependency. Incoming main repairs ordinary descriptor-reuse inode identity and KVM actual mount provenance, and puts the existing descriptor fixture in its owned temporary working directory. All fourteen real conflict resolutions are retained. Cargo/lock references keep the newer landed6ae; both historical and current budget explanations remain truthful.\n\nActual locked compilation and final Nextest enumeration measured **664 Hermit methods including every original630 and prior660**, **all six original SaBRe methods**, **681 Detcore methods**, and **508 regular methods**. Exactly four landed KVM configuration methods account for660→664. The four new native configuration methods and two affected existing stdio metadata methods actually passed. The extended CLI descriptor guest fixture was not executed in these checks. No original guest identity, acceptance oracle, filter or numeric runtime limit was dropped.\n\nThe source check scope completed0 in166.517 seconds with4CPU/8GiB/zero swap/1024 tasks. Actual all-target/all-feature workspace Clippy passed in11.250 seconds, together with fmt, pin/budget/refusal controls, shell syntax and generated-DAG checks. Peak memory reached8GiB with36,290 memory-max pressure events and zero OOM or task-limit event. The normal merge commit/hooks passed; all1,728 committed bytes/modes are bound to the final checked source checkpoint. Binary metadata still names precommit8a982+dirty; the official producer must establish committed-head prepared artifact authority.\n\nEarlier exact-source native evidence remains attached to its historical execution: the strengthened public-getrandom forwarding method,29 parser/snapshot methods, two real per-worker-TID raw-comm controls, and two exact installer helper controls against the actual6ae dependency. Installer helper/package evidence verifies required loader symbols, source revision and licenses, not a complete release installation. The unchanged controls were not rerun merely to obtain a new commit hash. The two newly authored valid-path refusal criteria were explicitly replaced with valid-path acceptance and wrong-object controls. Earlier compile/collector failures remain recorded.\n\nThe independent exact-head [Codex source/component review](https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3054#issuecomment-5712268469) approves c961 with explicit runtime limits. Actual independent Claude review of **c961** is still running. The earlier8a982 approvals remain historical, and the original refusal5710517195 was retired by its original issuer at https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3054#issuecomment-5711978105; that retirement is preserved, not republished as a new c961 approval.\n\nThe coordinator's official selected **664+6** attempt at c961 is currently active and explicitly off-record, using the original12-node closure and unchanged9,600 summed node-wall/10,800 whole-run bounds. No result, original DBT echo/true success, original-six success or canonical green claim is made. Latest retained canonical full run remains1838 at158a89f6, FAILED/NOT-VALIDATED with69 successful and10 unsuccessful node outcomes, completed2026-09-17 07:31 UTC. These historical full-run failures are not a new result at this head.\n\n## Relationship to gVisor\n\nThe carried KVM work concerns Hermit's namespace and guest-descriptor identity adapter; it does not replace gVisor's syscall implementation or certify new KVM runtime coverage here. The exact reverie-kvm subtree is identical between main's596b and retained6ae dependencies. Existing ptrace comparisons remain the required behavior reference.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\n**Triggers 2 and 3:** syscall-interception/bootstrap core abstraction and deterministic initial-state transfer. Human review follows the owner's post-facto P0 repair policy. This remains a draft pending the second exact-head review family and assessment of actual guest evidence. The source coauthor relays the independent report with attribution and does not self-attest or withdraw another reviewer's refusal.\n\nTask: hermit-main-tip-d0b1468b-not-validated-22-nodes-mostly-exit-2; owner dev-hermit. Final source evidence: assigned consumer slot `ignored/sabre-random-bootstrap-pin-20260917/merge-main-cdf-1/final-1`, main-relative patch SHA256 `2c26b8b3cb43706fb9250e1e5645a41ae204c74dfcd1aa3314547a673e456b0c`.\n","headRefName":"dev-hermit/sabre-random-bootstrap-pin-20260917","headRefOid":"c961f85503dee4629c00981a7707afe39da71a3e","isDraft":true,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","color":"FFA500"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO4w","name":"adversarial-review-codex1","description":"Codex adversarial review round 1 completed","color":"5319e7"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO-g","name":"adversarial-review-claude1","description":"Adversarial review pass, claude lane (round 1)","color":"1D76DB"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuG_rFw","name":"passed-review-claude","description":"Claude adversarial review PASSED at current PR head","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuHwd_w","name":"passed-review-codex","description":"Codex adversarial review PASSED at current PR head","color":"0E8A16"}],"number":3054,"state":"OPEN","title":"Preserve Detcore random state through SaBRe bootstrap","updatedAt":"2026-09-17T09:42:50Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3054"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"d0b1468b0964a16052bd32d90b9990aa0c9ace7a","body":"[hermit2, degraded-dev-hermit, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nThe 189 default portable compatibility commands currently run against host tools. Route those commands and their fixture producer through the pinned image, preserving every original payload, budget and result oracle. Give the image fixture an explicit pinned-toolchain mode and separate run-state directory so mixed host/image selections cannot overwrite each other's fixtures.\n\nTask: hermetic-validate-v3-clean-base-minimal-env-fixed-test-workdir\n\nThe image declares 153 executable paths, adds 19 links to locked package outputs and preserves the existing toolchain. Manifest syntax, duplicate/empty declarations and unavailable providers refuse. All 189 hosted counterparts and their 206-node closure remain unchanged. The image digest is `sha256:715ac8c96ef1267fb59f531d9885b91d5e1d5dfbb43126551187d7f6fb4ac633`; no Nix input or dependency pin was upgraded.\n\n## Validation and review\n\n- Focused Rust generator/selection controls, package all-target Clippy, workspace formatting, Python wrapper/cache controls and Nix positive/refusal controls passed on the exact committed source blobs.\n- Normal image build passed in 187.486s under 4 CPU/12GiB/zero swap. It reached the memory cap (76107 max events) with zero OOM/kill events; this is assembly evidence, not spare-memory headroom.\n- The actual image passed 153 declared-path and 140 PATH-command checks. A fresh read-only OLD8157 probe directly matched all 88 retained providers' resolved paths, modes, sizes and SHA256 against NEW715a in 1.443s, with no host mount over /nix.\n- Five actual fixture invocations passed: host/image sequential, host/image concurrent with measured overlap, and image recreation. Host bytes/inodes/mtimes remained unchanged. These were fixture controls with zero Hermit invocations.\n- Root native and independent Claude source reviews approve tree `b7f349b09b8be7c6a88e80c300651706203736d7`, exactly committed as `934032fab5bcdde908bccdee6fd99effc6f15c35`. Claude's initially unresolved old-provider hash provenance was subsequently resolved by the fresh 88-entry probe and independent root readback. Optional source observations remain documented; no review marker is synthesized.\n\n- Normal verified push passed changed-Rust formatting and the actual `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` hook. The whole hook/push operation took 89.446s under 4 CPU/8GiB/zero swap/1024tasks; no standalone Clippy-duration claim. Fresh remote readback confirms all nine blobs/modes and exact commit 934032fa.\n\n## Draft limits\n\nActual compatibility execution is pending. No full validation receipt or dependency-completeness credit is claimed. The original commands retain strict mode with legacy stripped verification (ptrace backend, default log level); this change makes no L2/bitwise-parity claim. No comparator, test assertion, node budget or workload case was weakened.\n\nThis is one part of the all-family hermetic task: full still has 32 unwrapped nodes and super 144. The task remains open. The proposed nine-node focused official-driver run is separately held for controller review and disk admission; the 211-node closure has not run. Detailed command/status/source-bound artifacts remain under the coordinator's `ignored/ci-hub/hermetic-v3-dev-hermit-20260916/` evidence tree.\n","headRefName":"dev-hermit/hermetic-v3-ready-20260916","headRefOid":"934032fab5bcdde908bccdee6fd99effc6f15c35","isDraft":true,"labels":[],"number":3030,"state":"OPEN","title":"Run default compatibility checks in the pinned image","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T14:29:39Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3030"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"2bd68e12a5a7da9c6e9f0666072df925126af9fc","body":"[hermit2, recon-2, claude-opus-5, devbig014, role=impl]\n\nValidate run 1819 recorded **all 26** of its failed nodes as `product_failure`,\nincluding **17** whose own detail was verbatim *\"required structured test\nresults were not written\"*. Nineteen of those refused before executing a single\ntest. A consumer counting product failures from that row gets 26 where the\nevidence supports 6 — and the scorecard's Red definition depends on exactly the\ndistinction that row cannot make.\n\n## One cause of three, not the whole prefix\n\n`dagrun` funnels three conditions into one refusal string at\n`scheduler.rs::run_step`:\n\n| reason | meaning | class |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cannot read structured test results <path>` | file exists, unreadable | product |\n| `malformed structured test results <path>` | file exists, content wrong | product |\n| `required structured test results were not written` | **nothing exists** | infrastructure |\n\nThe first two are statements about a report the run produced, so the suite is a\nfair suspect — a duplicate test identity is a malformed suite. Only the third\nsays no evidence exists in either direction, and only the third is reclassified.\n\nThat is why `product_evidence_bracket`'s existing *\"duplicate test identity\"*\nassertion keeps passing **untouched**: its fixture is the malformed case.\n\n`NodeClassification::result` already maps `UnderstoodInfrastructureFailure` to\n`no_result`, which is both halves of the requirement — such a node stops\ncounting as red **and** cannot count as green evidence for landing.\n\n## Presence of evidence still beats absence\n\n`has_product_failure_evidence` checks measured failing tests first and\nindependently, so a node that reported a failed test and *then* failed to write\nits report is still a product failure. Only absence is reclassified. Asserted\nboth ways.\n\n## ⚠️ My first version of that assertion could not fail\n\nI built the never-written fixture by cloning `infra`, which already carries\n`understood_infrastructure_class = \"PMU RCB overshoot\"` and therefore classifies\nas infrastructure through a *different* arm — so the assertion passed with the\nrepair disabled. The control is the only reason I know. It now builds from a\nbase with no infrastructure diagnosis, so the reclassification is the only thing\nthat can satisfy it.\n\nThat is the same defect this change exists to fix, reproduced inside its own\nevidence.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `./scripts/validate.rs --self-test` **PASSED** with the change\n- **Controlled**: with the reclassification disabled, the same self-test\n  **FAILS** — `classification: a result that was never written was reported as a\n  product failure`, exit 1\n- `rustfmt --edition 2024 --check` clean\n\n## Scope\n\nThree mechanisms, three fixes. This makes a **future** refusal from any cause\nread honestly. It does **not** stop the refusals — that is\n[hermit#3026](https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3026), which moves the\ntemporary summaries out of prepared source identity. It does **not** touch the\npost-run scorecard write-back.\n","headRefName":"recon-2/classify-never-written-as-infrastructure","headRefOid":"ec8917a695bc4b436729c98dddb064e0a16c0137","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO-g","name":"adversarial-review-claude1","description":"Adversarial review pass, claude lane (round 1)","color":"1D76DB"}],"number":3027,"state":"OPEN","title":"Classify a never-written test result as infrastructure, not product","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T22:28:14Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3027"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"0ac76b5e2fc0ec0711a88f78231d6fe1d67858da","body":"[hermit2, series-rows, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\nPlain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nHermit's full validation currently cannot produce a qualifying receipt on main: the Rust-script build starts eight Cargo jobs inside a 2 GiB cgroup and was OOM-killed, while the completion audit also rejects integration tests that the DAG executes without typed declarations. This restores a runnable, auditable graph without skipping or relabelling any gate.\n\nTask\n\nThis is the validation prerequisite for https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3009. It lowers all five generated Rust-script producer variants from eight to two Cargo jobs while retaining the existing 2 GiB cap and CARGO_BUILD_JOBS channel. It adds the missing `analyze` and `liteinst_advanced` declarations and teaches the audit parser that the existing `prlimit` command is an execution wrapper.\n\nDeterminism\n\nNo scheduler, guest execution, or record/replay behavior changes. The DAG retains the same commands, dependencies, and validation gates; only build parallelism metadata and truthful integration-test registration change.\n\nLinux Semantics\n\nNo Linux syscall or process semantics change. `prlimit` is recognized only by the static audit parser as the wrapper already present in a validation command.\n\nValidation\n\n- canonical DAG generation and `--check` passed\n- all 15 `validation_dag` tests passed\n- all 13 audit-parser mutation tests passed\n- registration audit passed with 55 registered binaries and zero undeclared\n- `scripts/validate.rs --self-test` passed under required cgroup containment in 5m45s\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, Python compilation, and `git diff --check` passed\n","headRefName":"validation-prereq-series-rows-20260914","headRefOid":"1f26de2c6a8291076a87019c31fbb1037386282b","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO4w","name":"adversarial-review-codex1","description":"Codex adversarial review round 1 completed","color":"5319e7"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuHwd_w","name":"passed-review-codex","description":"Codex adversarial review PASSED at current PR head","color":"0E8A16"}],"number":3010,"state":"OPEN","title":"Repair full-validation graph prerequisites","updatedAt":"2026-09-14T07:55:49Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3010"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"0ac76b5e2fc0ec0711a88f78231d6fe1d67858da","body":"[hermit2, slots-recon, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nNative control runs now retain the ordered process outcome and full observation hash for every inner attempt, together with the producer-owned diversity requirement and observed count. This lets canonical reporting show why the control passed and keeps identical successful observations as an auditable insufficient-diversity failure rather than mislabeling them as a product crash.\n\nTask: preserve_native_attempt_observation\n\nThe change introduces CellResult schema 5 and stress-series/v4, validates the narrow native evidence shape and its outer classification, keeps older series rows readable, and updates exact-schema result consumers. Missing, partial, reordered, malformed, or contradictory evidence is refused.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe representation preserves producer output in attempt order and recomputes distinct hashes with a set only for cardinality. It introduces no host time, random value, iteration-order dependency, or guest-visible behavior.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThis changes result recording and classification only. Guest execution and Linux process behavior are unchanged; status, signal, and timeout observations are retained exactly.\n\n## Validation\n\nAt exact head `086ec9b3c423127c4980a41df435c39a1a23c0d1`:\n\n- `cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan`: 222 passed\n- `./scripts/validate.rs --self-test`: passed\n- `./ci/compat-envelope/scorecard.rs self-test`: passed\n- `./ci/compat-envelope/scorecard.rs check`: passed\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`: passed\n\nThe parent projection and website suites are validated in the coordinated dev-hermit change.\n","headRefName":"preserve-native-attempt-observation-20260914","headRefOid":"e26c832e98e23eff3044c874467c0a991827c20f","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuHwd_w","name":"passed-review-codex","description":"Codex adversarial review PASSED at current PR head","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACu2t1Sw","name":"adversarial-review-codex3","description":"Codex adversarial review round 3 conducted","color":"BFD4F2"}],"number":3009,"state":"OPEN","title":"Preserve native diversity evidence","updatedAt":"2026-09-15T21:05:29Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3009"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"3111e75531c175e00dea7f6e1758bd9791019b82","body":"[hermit2, mega-lander, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nThis cuts ptrace, KVM, and LiteInst verification cells over from Hermit's internal two-run mode to two ordinary `hermit run --run-evidence-dir` processes managed by the manifest harness. The runs are independently schedulable under the existing global process cap, publish typed evidence, and are compared in-process with `BitwiseInfoV1`. A successful cell retains exactly run 1's canonical log as a verified deterministic gzip plus typed descriptor and outer-ledger binding, then removes both raw logs.\n\nBefore this change, these cells delegated pair ownership and comparison to the CLI's internal verifier. After it, the harness owns process scheduling, evidence validation, comparison, retention, restart accounting, and failure classification while public `hermit run` behavior remains unchanged unless the new harness-only flags are selected.\n\nTask: `flip-verify-to-two-harness-managed-runs-with-retained-logs`\n\n## Determinism\n\nEach pair shares one immutable prepared guest but has distinct no-clobber work, capture, result, stdout, stderr, and evidence paths. The harness waits for both terminal processes, then accepts only complete typed sidecars with nonzero canonical INFO records and matching size/digest metadata. Missing, empty, truncated, malformed, unreadable, replaced, aliased, or digest-mismatched evidence yields `no_result`, never success. `BitwiseInfoV1` removes only the real wall-clock prefix and ordinalizes host addresses; all remaining INFO payload bytes and guest status/stdout/stderr are exact.\n\nRetry remains restricted to the existing typed `ProductFailure` rule and repeats a complete pair. Retention recomputes compressed and uncompressed bytes, sizes, and digests, publishes exactly one run-1 gzip and descriptor durably, binds it into schema-10 outer history, and removes the two raw canonical logs only after publication.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThe default CLI, public `--log-file`, exit codes, and ordinary guest descriptors are unchanged. The discoverable harness-only `--run-result-json`, `--guest-stdout`, and `--guest-stderr` options require `--run-evidence-dir` and use unique no-clobber regular files held relative to the claimed directory. Ptrace and LiteInst install those preopened guest descriptors after Reverie's container setup so Hermit's own diagnostics remain separate; controls cover stdout/stderr/status, descriptor behavior, session/process-group identity, aliasing, and path replacement.\n\nSaBRe intentionally remains on its named legacy internal-verifier path because its plugin DETLOG is available only through shared stderr. DBT remains absent/prelaunch-refused because its current evidence-file mechanism implies isolated process groups and would change guest `setsid`/`setpgid` behavior. Neither path is presented as equivalent.\n\n## Relationship to gVisor\n\nNo gVisor or KVM execution semantics change. KVM uses the same typed sidecar and canonical comparison contract, but guest output is copied from the existing virtual-console result and the report continues to state the existing exit-code-only disposition limitation; it does not invent signal precision.\n\n## Validation\n\nExact head: `e7af5cd8e5a80a0b74be5f44f26b33d4798c2697`, stacked on PR #2968 exact head `841fe4f6857c19f4e3bcfe135fb2601da2c8f925`.\n\n- `cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan --all-targets`: 277 passed.\n- `cargo test -p hermit --bin hermit`: 240 passed.\n- `cargo test -p hermit --test run_evidence`: 10 passed.\n- `rustup run nightly rust-script --test scripts/validate.rs`: 47 passed.\n- `with-proxy rustup run nightly rust-script scripts/validate.rs --self-test`: passed in a clean clone at the exact head; 12/259 constructed self-test steps selected, 259-node fused plan, expected zero real DAG nodes.\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p hermit -p hermit-manifest-plan --all-targets -- -D warnings`: passed.\n- Ptrace `c-programs/session-identity`: L2 `BitwiseInfoV1` pass, 128/128 INFO, status 0, no relaxations.\n- LiteInst `c-programs/name-to-handle-regular-eopnotsupp`: L2 `BitwiseInfoV1` pass, 1144/1144 INFO, status 0, no relaxations; guest stdout/stderr remained separate from Hermit diagnostics.\n- KVM `c-programs/session-identity`: L2 `BitwiseInfoV1` pass, 118/118 INFO, status 0, no relaxations, subject to the named KVM disposition limitation above.\n- Each exact-head real control retained exactly one run-1 gzip and no raw canonical log.\n\nThe broader `cargo test -p hermit --all-targets` still has the pre-existing `record_replay_stable_arbitrary_binaries` gettimeofday-policy refusal. The identical command fails at untouched base `841fe4f` on the same host in the same way; this patch does not weaken that policy.\n\nOne first clean-clone self-test attempt hit a transient rustc/LLVM 23.1 SIGSEGV during codegen. It was not an OOM and did not reproduce: an unchanged feature retry, the untouched-base control, and the exact committed-head self-test all passed with the same toolchain/default jobs.\n","headRefName":"feature/harness-managed-run-evidence-v1","headRefOid":"e7af5cd8e5a80a0b74be5f44f26b33d4798c2697","isDraft":true,"labels":[],"number":2969,"state":"OPEN","title":"Use harness-managed run evidence for verification","updatedAt":"2026-09-13T10:54:17Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2969"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"500dded1b2c614694ba97bdafe2d196c8ed63eb4","body":"[hermit2, mega-lander, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nThis draft prepares the validation harness to replace internal `--verify-json` execution with two harness-managed ordinary runs whose evidence can be retained and audited. It centralizes the BitwiseInfoV1 comparison in the shared Rust library, adds typed ordinary-run evidence for supported paths, implements deterministic retained-log publication with bounded and restart-safe accounting, and preserves a reviewed continuation scheduler for the later atomic cutover.\n\nThis does not activate the cutover. The live harness still uses the existing single `--verify --verify-json` command. DBT exact guest stdout/stderr still requires a DynamoRIO core-level separation, SaBRe still lacks an authoritative ordinary-run log channel, and schema/scorecard/retained-artifact integration remains outstanding.\n\nTask: flip-verify-to-two-harness-managed-runs-with-retained-logs\n\n## Determinism\n\nAll comparison callers use the same fixed BitwiseInfoV1 implementation in `detcore::logdiff`. The retained gzip has deterministic metadata and is bound to the exact compared bytes by raw and compressed hashes, sizes, peer digest, cell identity, attempt, and selected INFO count. Publication is transactional, budgeted with checked arithmetic, restart-reconciled, and reviewed against path replacement, partial writes, crashes, aliases, and concurrent writers. The active validation path is intentionally unchanged until every backend prerequisite is sound.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThis draft does not change guest syscall semantics. Ordinary ptrace capture preserves exact guest status/stdout/stderr. DBT ordinary log decoding now rejects authenticated backend/tool failures before publication and removes transport-only initialization records after validating the protected evidence; DBT typed stream capture remains refused because launcher/core and guest fd1/fd2 are not structurally separated. SaBRe remains refused for the same evidence-quality reason.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan --all-targets`: 249 passed\n- `cargo test -p hermit --features dbt --bin hermit`: 242 passed\n- Detcore-model canonical verdict tests: 19 passed\n- Detcore BitwiseInfoV1 tests: 11 passed\n- Cross-backend script self-test: 46 passed\n- Real ptrace retained pair: 323/323 selected INFO records, matched\n- Real DBT retained pair: 9/9 selected INFO records, matched\n- Ptrace-to-DBT and DBT-to-ptrace controls: both diverge at record 5 as expected; no parity or L2 claim\n- Formatting, warning-denied Clippy, pin validation, shell syntax, diff, and repository-cleanliness checks passed\n\nBroader DBT CLI validation remains 106/113; the seven failures are existing DBT or feature-matrix limitations reproduced or classified separately. This draft is not ready to merge.\n","headRefName":"feature/verify-two-harness-managed-runs-v2","headRefOid":"f18b76cc464a3f8f7704f2d7abdbf53baff157e0","isDraft":true,"labels":[],"number":2958,"state":"OPEN","title":"Prepare harness-managed verify evidence","updatedAt":"2026-09-05T10:26:46Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2958"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"54ea4e3935a86cbb0257fcbe2debf4ba620e5035","body":"[hermit2, lander-2, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nFull validation currently records aggregate test totals after the producer-owned per-test files have been deleted. This change retains the terminal result and retry count for every counted test as canonical content-addressed evidence, keeps direct compatibility results in their existing separate population, and binds the checked totals and artifact digest into the typed validation ledger row. Missing, malformed, duplicated, count-only, or inconsistent evidence now prevents a schema-8 receipt instead of leaving totals that cannot be independently recomputed.\n\nIt also corrects the stale-base refusal text without weakening admission. A stale head is still refused because it omits main changes available at launch; the message now accurately distinguishes exact-commit validation evidence from the separate hard-green or soft-green decision made at the landing boundary.\n\nTasks: `retain_exact_per_test`, `correct_hermit_validate_stale`\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe artifact is serialized from producer-owned terminal results only. Nodes and per-producer test identities are sorted before serialization, the bytes end in exactly one newline, and SHA-256 binds those canonical bytes to the ledger evidence. Checked recomputation sums each selected terminal node plus the separate compatibility population exactly once. Older outer attempts are excluded only by the existing terminal-attempt selection.\n\nNo runtime or guest-visible behavior changes.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo Linux syscall, scheduling, filesystem, or process semantics change. This affects validation evidence retention and one admission diagnostic only.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `rust-script --force scripts/validate.rs --self-test` — PASS at `cefe94340d721d537f7afc9398eaa5a2285740a4`; `FINAL_VALIDATE_STATUS PASSED`; 2m11s. This covers canonical 4/3/7 recomputation; terminal-attempt selection; count-only, missing, no-result, duplicate, truncated, corrupt-digest, and spoofed-total refusal; and stale-base refusal with the corrected wording.\n- `cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan` — PASS, all 205 tests across targets.\n- `cargo check -p hermit-manifest-plan` — PASS.\n- `cargo clippy -p hermit-manifest-plan --all-targets -- -D warnings` — PASS.\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — PASS.\n\nAssurance: L0 infrastructure validation. Backend: none; log level: default; relaxations: none.\n\n## Consumers\n\n- `scripts/validate.rs` selects established terminal attempts, retains the canonical artifact, emits schema 8 only when evidence is complete, and serializes the typed ledger evidence.\n- `ci/manifest-plan/src/ledger.rs::HistoryRow` is the shared typed producer/reader boundary for the new evidence.\n- `compat_test_results` remains the producer for the separate `compatibility` population.\n- `run_test_counts` remains the aggregate whose totals are checked against recomputation.\n- `validate`'s isolated clean-checkout self-test copies and stages both the new module and the modified ledger type, so nested compilation exercises the real files.\n- Downstream receipt and landing readers consume the complete ledger row; their schema-8 verification is intentionally a dependent change rather than inferred in this producer patch.\n","headRefName":"fix/retain-exact-per-test-receipt","headRefOid":"cefe94340d721d537f7afc9398eaa5a2285740a4","isDraft":false,"labels":[],"number":2922,"state":"OPEN","title":"Retain exact validation test results","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T01:35:31Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2922"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"move-demos-directory-into-hermit","baseRefOid":"1eef513dc8a9d97d910206fe3f72bfb28a67ea62","body":"[hermit2, demo08review, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nDemo 8 demonstrates Hermit's headline capability on a real program: chaos scheduling finds a use-after-free in `btrfs-convert` that blind execution misses, and replays it. It worked only for someone who already knew a crashing seed. Every seed recorded in the tree was wrong at the current head, the calibration that would have derived a fresh one **could not run at all**, and a stale seed reported itself as a regression in the fix.\n\n**Before:** a reader with a clean tree got either `SKIPPED`, or exit 1 claiming the bug did not reproduce.\n**After:** one command derives a seed for the inputs actually present and prints the ASAN report; a stale record is named as stale and re-derived rather than blamed on the fixture.\n\nBase is `move-demos-directory-into-hermit` (PR #2904), not `main`, because Demo 8 does not exist on `main`. Follow-up to the independent review of that PR — verdict at https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2904#issuecomment-5485325069.\n\n⚠️ **This PR carries no `Demo-Green-Review` trailer, deliberately.** I performed the review that produced these findings, so I do not certify that my own fix meets them. A separate lane owns that. The commit was made with `HERMIT_DEMO_REVIEW_OVERRIDE=1`, the documented pre-review path, which does not exempt the merge.\n\n## What was broken\n\n**1. Calibration could not run.** `scripts/hermit-box-run` looked for a runner only at `agent-utils/bin/dagrun` and `agent-utils/bin/safe-ci-dag-runner`. The pinned `agent-utils` `0cdc8867` has no top-level `bin/`; `dagrun` is at `agent-utils/common/bin/dagrun`. Every invocation died with exit 2 before running anything — measured, 0 of 8 calibration seeds executed. The lookup now tries `common/bin` and `rs/bin` first, and a not-found result exits **3** rather than 2, because for a caller that is the same fact as \"this host cannot box\", and callers already branch on 3 to degrade to an announced unboxed run.\n\n**2. The calibrator and the demo disagreed about what counts as a crash.** The calibrator detects on the ASAN report text; the demo gated on exit status first. Seed 3 reports the UAF and still exits 0, and it is the lowest crashing seed here — so an ascending sweep selected exactly the seed the demo rejected as \"did not crash\". Both now use the report text, and the calibrator prefers a seed whose report reached its `SUMMARY` line, because that is the report Step 4 compares.\n\n**3. A stale seed read as a regression.** The seed is recorded as `<seed> <fixture-sha256> <hermit-sha256>` and **verified by replay** before use.\n\n### On the third field, which the owner asked about explicitly\n\nMaking the Hermit hash load-bearing is *useful but not sufficient*, and it is **not** the gate here. Measured at `00ed139b`: seeds 3, 6, 10 and 13 reproduce while 15, 17 and 19 do not, against a **fixture-identical** binary. That establishes only that *the fixture alone does not determine the seed set* — **not** that the Hermit build does. No older Hermit was built to isolate the variable, and host, kernel and toolchain were uncontrolled too. A hash gate would assume the answer to that open question, and would still vouch for a stale seed if the deciding input is one nobody hashed; it also discards good seeds whenever a release rebuild is not bit-reproducible. Replaying the seed is an *observation* rather than an inference, and holds whichever input changed. The hashes are kept because they make the recalibration reason legible.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe demo and the calibrator were running **without `--strict`**, and that was a real defect rather than a formality. `btrfs-convert` generates a random UUID for the target filesystem, which non-strict Hermit does not virtualize:\n\n| Mode | Runs | Target UUID | UAF reproduced |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| non-strict | 3 | **two different UUIDs** | — |\n| `--strict` | 4 | `10708a9d-…` every time | **4/4** |\n\nWithin a single non-strict demo run, seed 6 reproduced the UAF at Step 2 and **missed** at Step 4, the two transcripts differing at the UUID line. Cost of `--strict` here is ~9 s per run against ~7 s. Both scripts now pass `--strict` and use one identical flag set, since a seed derived under one flag set is not evidence about another.\n\nStep 4 previously compared a four-line filtered ASAN extract, which does not contain the UUID — so it would have reported \"byte-identical\" across the two runs that actually differed. It now compares the **entire guest transcript** with only host-side lines removed: safehermit's accounting, and Hermit's own log lines, which begin with a real UTC timestamp. That is the rule the repository's comparison policy already applies to retained logs. 93 lines are compared, both UUID lines and the full ASAN report among them.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo guest-visible behaviour changes. The fixtures, the pinned btrfs-progs commit, the patch, and the observability adaptation are untouched; only which seed is selected, how it is verified, and what is compared.\n\n## Validation\n\ndevbig014, AMD EPYC 9D85. Backend **ptrace**, log level **error**, single relaxation **`--no-virtualize-cpuid`** (CPUID faulting unavailable on the demo hosts). Hermit release built at `00ed139b`, sha256 `7d559e36…`. All Hermit runs through `bin/safehermit`.\n\n- **Cold tree, no recorded seed, one command** — derives seed 6, reproduces the UAF at `task_period_wait` (`common/task-utils.c:154`) from `print_copied_inodes` (`convert/main.c:169`), fixed variant clean with the progress path engaged, replay byte-identical. **GREEN.**\n- **Stale record** (seed 17, correct fixture hash, foreign Hermit hash) — reported as `STALE SEED RECORD`, explicitly not a regression; re-derived seed 6. **GREEN.**\n- `scripts/test-prepare-demo08-calibration.sh` — **PASS**. It previously asserted on machinery `ea4840f15` had removed and exited 1 with **zero bytes of output**, so nothing it claimed had ever been checked. Six brackets now, including a stale cached seed and a truncated-vs-complete report preference.\n- `make -C demos test` — 36 passed. `./scripts/test-check-demo-review.sh` — 6 passed. `./scripts/check-checker-scheduling.rs` — OK. `shellcheck --severity=warning` clean on all six changed shell scripts.\n\nNot run here: `make lint-checks` in full, and the P0 demo gate.\n\n## Also in this change\n\n- `demos/clean.sh` is now a Demo 8 consumer — it removes the recorded seed, the one file whose staleness manufactures a false regression, and no longer exits early when the QEMU asset directory is absent.\n- `demos/run-all.sh` records a gated-out demo as `SKIP` rather than `PASS`. It still does not fail the sweep.\n- `demos/README.md` described a calibration contract the code had stopped implementing; restoring the replay makes it true again instead of deleting the promise.\n- `scripts/check-checker-scheduling.rs` allowlist reason updated to the truth. Scheduling that bracket would make the lint node depend on an ASAN runtime, because it refuses rather than skipping when it cannot build its control — that is an owner decision, left visible rather than armed unilaterally.\n","headRefName":"demo-08-derive-crash-seed","headRefOid":"68605f886745a060e0a2a8e24ab933815acc2d22","isDraft":false,"labels":[],"number":2907,"state":"OPEN","title":"demos: derive Demo 8's crash seed, and make a stale one say so","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T02:00:10Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2907"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"6f2a46ffbf0854f99b10109f34b653ef86594cac","body":"[hermit2, dagrun, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nMove the unified demo suite into Hermit so its workflows run entirely inside the public repository on Hermit's available self-hosted runners. The existing Hermit demo directory is merged rather than replaced, and the imported suite keeps its filtered source history.\n\nThe moved demos resolve Hermit at the repository root, use in-repository support scripts, route direct Hermit execution through `bin/safehermit`, and fetch the pinned QEMU kernel from a public rrnewton/hermit release. The Demo Hot Path no longer needs a private dev-hermit checkout.\n\nTask: move-demos-directory-into-hermit\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe move preserves the existing fixed inputs, exact binary selection, content hashes, repeat comparisons, and serialized artifact publication. Demo 2 deliberately builds and names the debug Hermit binary: the same source's release binary records successfully but replay exits 125 after the bootstrap exec returns EFAULT. That release defect is not relabelled as a pass.\n\nDemo 7 now runs through safehermit, identifies QEMU by its unique QMP argument across the wrapper's process boundary, and stops the exact transient unit before waiting for the wrapper's complete report.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo Hermit syscall or guest semantics change. The suite's existing process, QEMU, snapshot, and guest commands are retained; only repository ownership, dependency paths, and validation wiring change.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Demos 1-7: PASS at exact head ancestry, independently reviewed. Demo 2 used the documented debug binary qualification.\n- Demo 7 after the safehermit fix: two reproducible 84-to-85 task-list evolutions, complete wrapper reports, both exact transient services unloaded, no surviving matching processes.\n- Demo unit tests: 48/48 PASS.\n- Demo review checker regression tests: 15/15 PASS.\n- `bin/safehermit-test.sh`: 16/16 PASS.\n- Shellcheck, Python compilation, YAML parsing, dependency checks, and public kernel digest verification: PASS.\n- `make lint-checks`: PASS before the final rebase; after rebasing, the previously external checker-scheduling main red is repaired by landed main commit `7e4613564` and the live checker passes.\n- Full `make validate` previously stopped before changed code because `build.rust_scripts` failed; the same workspace error was reproduced on untouched base `2738b00dd`. The newer base has not been claimed fully validated here.\n\nDemo 8 was deliberately not executed by this agent. The demo-review gate now requires coverage per PATH and refuses an attestation older than the last change to that path, so a partial trailer is no longer mechanically accepted and the requirement no longer needs restating here. At the current head the gate is honestly RED at rc=1 with 20 stale runnable paths, awaiting a distinct reviewer's trailer; the author must not self-attest.\n\nAssurance: demos 1-7 runtime evidence only. Backend and relaxations are those declared by each demo; no broader Hermit L-level claim is made.\n\n","headRefName":"move-demos-directory-into-hermit","headRefOid":"33a8f546deac001a2ae41f1a1706a0c9f9fc9b85","isDraft":false,"labels":[],"number":2904,"state":"OPEN","title":"demos: move the unified suite into Hermit","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T23:57:11Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2904"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"eec5e18affd17229f86b3943520ee2b9821a377d","body":"[hermit2, degraded-unresolved, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\nUnverified --who metadata: dev-hermit\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nSaBRe could finish two otherwise matching runs with different INFO shutdown events because the host's physical-exit report raced the scheduler's completion check. The backend now records that report separately, and scheduler maintenance applies it under the same lock used to decide completion. Consuming the logical child wait cannot erase an unprocessed report or make the child waitable twice.\n\nThis change addresses the race between the scheduler's loop-top completion check and its step2 completion check. It does not establish that the broader SaBRe divergence in a guest that forks and reaps with wait4(WNOHANG) is fixed; that investigation remains open. The retained root-process and sched-setattr workloads qualify the completion-report ordering described here. They do not qualify the separate fork/reap workload recorded in the scheduler comment.\n\nThis recovers the remaining scheduler and all-feature diagnostic work from the original 38-path, 19-commit proposal on current main. Its validation graph, typed termination, prepared Nextest and CPU-evidence work is already preserved through https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2970, https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2822, https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2833 and https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2966. The current single DAG, width limits, dependency pin, result schemas, selected cells and measurement history remain intact. The original public source is preserved separately before replacement.\n\nTask: `astra-lane-drains-hermit-reverie-pr-queues-to-zero`.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Keep physical exit reporting separate from scheduler application, including report-before-wait and wait-before-report orderings and exec identity reuse.\n- Retain the three original scheduler tests and add a public-callback/actual-scheduler completion control.\n- Preserve all INFO events and strengthen both retained SaBRe log checks with the original zero-fizzle assertion.\n- Restore the original backend diagnostic assertions under all features. Update only the measured Detcore test count from 667 to 671; all other generated DAG values remain unchanged.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe asynchronous callback records a fact without choosing a scheduler completion outcome. The scheduler drains reports under its lock immediately before the loop-top completion check. Existing physical-exit barriers, pending admission, waiter/timer logic and child membership remain authoritative. A duplicate begin retains its pending report; a fresh incarnation clears stale completion state.\n\nThe strict BitwiseInfoV1 policy, complete INFO record comparison, I/O buffers, virtual time, exact remainder, selected cells, CPU/wall limits and retry policy are unchanged. No log is hidden or demoted. The separate waiter-liveness proposal at https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2747 is not included.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThe supervisor still observes the final kernel wait status. Guest wait ownership and status consumption remain separate from the backend physical report: consuming a wait cannot erase that report, and applying a report cannot resurrect a consumed child. Current child-TID gating and signal-mask ABI behavior from https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2985 are preserved. SaBRe is the enabled reporting backend; this is not a claim about unresolved KVM physical-exit or signal work.\n\n## Relationship to gVisor\n\nThis change does not use or modify gVisor.\n\n## Validation\n\nCandidate: `1948e95fe14cc95687e886704420a9922a1fe1cb`, based on main `eec5e18affd17229f86b3943520ee2b9821a377d`.\n\n- Native combined Detcore population: 671 passed, zero failed/ignored/filtered; 15.904 s including build. All 667 main identities and metadata remain, with exactly four additions. Final Clippy with warnings denied, formatting and graph freshness passed. The graph's 1,388 nodes differ only in that exact count.\n- The current-source public callback fails before the repair. Restoring the missing maintenance drain or deleting a pending report causes the named controls to fail; the defective all-feature diagnostic also fails the original assertion. All old failures remain retained.\n- Contained build: all 15 commands passed, with 16 GiB memory, zero swap, eight CPU equivalents, 4,096 tasks and bounded captures. Actual compiled SHA is `1948e95fe14c`; published Hermit SHA256 is `b3765c35a181536b40755dd04ef0d08735a329230e279a37ea8c6a13974b15da`.\n- Official `c-programs/sched-setattr-batch` verify/SaBRe qualification: 20/20 on attempt one, 5.948 s total, maximum width 8 and per-harness width 1. Every run retains CPU 22 s/wall 57 s. All 40 raw logs have exactly one scheduler completion before fallback completion and no fizzle event. Each comparison contains 103 INFO records per side, with full canonical policy, equal I/O and no relaxation. Both executions in every pair record guest RPC activity, zero ptrace fallback sites and zero trusted shared-object sites. Maximum observed cell CPU was 535,493 us and wall was 690 ms.\n- Existing five-test `test.sabre_examples` node: local off-record driver exit zero in 97.818 s; node wall 60.605 s, Nextest execution 9.805 s. The retained typed CPU/result reconciliation records exactly the five expected identities, each attempt one and exit zero. Prepared executable/runtime hashes and the existing node CPU 7,200 s/wall 900 s remain intact. The Nextest build-script-output warnings are retained; no test was skipped.\n\nThe focused run published no receipt or ledger row. These results do not claim full-main validation, repeat the original width-20 campaign, or turn the historical full-profile receipt into current evidence. Complete original source accounting and immutable build/native/guest artifacts are recorded in the root TaskGraph notes.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\nPost-facto human review trigger 4 applies: this changes when core Detcore scheduling incorporates an asynchronously reported physical process exit. Exact candidate and base are stated above. The all-feature diagnostic and measured-count updates do not introduce another trigger.\n","headRefName":"degraded-ci-hub/sabre-shutdown-order","headRefOid":"1948e95fe14cc95687e886704420a9922a1fe1cb","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","color":"FFA500"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO-g","name":"adversarial-review-claude1","description":"Adversarial review pass, claude lane (round 1)","color":"1D76DB"}],"number":2836,"state":"OPEN","title":"Apply SaBRe physical-exit reports during scheduler maintenance","updatedAt":"2026-09-15T23:35:04Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2836"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"bae4a34fec5bfe45dd8cad00adcd5b6ddb04abcc","body":"[hermit2, hermit-125, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nSaBRe could let a sibling enter `pthread_join` after Detcore's logical thread exit but before Linux completed the physical thread exit and cleared `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`. Identical executions then observed different futex outcomes. This change keeps siblings parked until the SaBRe ptrace supervisor reports the final kernel exit status, then performs the existing logical child-TID wake.\n\nThe measured mechanism covers `backend-parity-c/pthread-lifecycle`, `chaos-c/lock-granularity`, and `c-programs/ppoll-simulation`. It is separate from the DBT `wait4` work in https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/506 and https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2737.\n\nTask: `pthread-exit-join-timing-accounts-for-two-of-the-eight-divergences`\n\n## Summary\n\n- Record an ordinary SaBRe thread exit before returning control to that thread.\n- Keep sibling threads parked until the SaBRe supervisor observes the final kernel exit status.\n- Perform the existing child-TID futex wake at that point; retain the immediate wake on backends without physical-exit reporting.\n- Cover the ordering and the unchanged backend behavior with scheduler unit tests.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe ordinary `Exit { group: false }` turn installs the wait before the exiting thread resumes. While that wait exists, no sibling receives a scheduler turn. The existing child-TID futex wake is delayed until the backend reports the exact thread's final kernel exit status. Completion removes the wait and wakes the joiner. The ordering is therefore fixed by the deterministic exit turn and the kernel's actual completion of that exit, rather than by host timing between those events.\n\nThe canonical comparator, its assertions, tolerances, labels, and gates are unchanged.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nLinux clears `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` and wakes its futex during final thread exit. The change waits for that physical boundary before allowing a modeled `pthread_join` wake, so a resumed joiner cannot race Linux's write. Backends without physical-exit reporting retain the existing immediate logical wake behavior.\n\n## Validation\n\nExact-head evidence used Hermit `76b078dd5e64e5a3bdfec8d6d187db0f1094dca5`, based on `d4d9fe5effe31a90c5a64238ce99fc5ddeea4710`, with pinned Reverie `ab07a89239150df3726a036bee9f5e897893dfc1`, kernel `6.13.2-0_fbk17_hardened_0_g2ae417e0caa0`, and capability masks `Inh=0`, `Prm=0`, `Eff=0`, `Bnd=000001ffffffffff`, `Amb=0`. All 65 typed rows report the exact Hermit source SHA, a clean source tree, binary build SHA `76b078dd5e64`, the SaBRe backend, INFO logs, no relaxations, and the canonical `BitwiseInfoV1` comparison.\n\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed.\n- `cargo test -p hermit-detcore --lib`: 566 passed, 0 failed.\n- `cargo clippy -p hermit-detcore --lib -- -D warnings`: passed.\n- SaBRe-only release build: passed; `hermit --version` reports `g76b078dd5e64`.\n- `backend-parity-c/pthread-lifecycle/verify@sabre`: 20 matched of 20. Both retained logs have the final ordinary child exit on scheduler turn 13, main-thread `FutexWait` on turn 14, and futex syscall 26 returning `Ok(0)`.\n- `chaos-c/lock-granularity/verify@sabre`: the former exit/join difference occurred in 0 of 20 comparisons. Nineteen comparisons matched completely. One diverged earlier, at the first child's start on scheduler turn 2, before any child exit; both retained logs later have the ordinary child exit on turn 7, main-thread `FutexWait` on turn 8, and futex syscall 16 returning `Ok(0)`. This change is not credited with that separate earlier timing difference.\n- `c-programs/ppoll-simulation/verify@sabre`: 20 matched of 20. After ppoll syscall 22, both retained logs have main-thread syscall 23 as stdout `fstat`; neither execution enters the formerly differing join futex at that point.\n- Control `system-utils/startup-tls-guards/verify@sabre`: 5 diverged of 5 at scheduler turn 3 and main-thread write syscall 49, with the stack canary and pointer guard differing. This shows the unchanged canonical comparison still reports a real divergence and that this change does not claim the separate startup TLS cause.\n\nThe harness classified each SaBRe row as failed because its separate execution-path qualification does not accept trusted libc sites. The retained path evidence nevertheless reports a complete path, guest RPC observed, and zero ptrace fallback sites. The counts above are the typed strict-comparison verdicts, not the harness process exit status.\n\nThe pre-fix evidence is identified by event content, not record position: pthread-lifecycle and lock-granularity differ across an ordinary child `Exit`, the following main-thread `FutexWait`, and the join futex result. The independently reproduced ppoll case in https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2745 differs after ppoll syscall 22 when main-thread syscall 23 is the pthread-join futex in one execution and stdout `fstat` in the other.\n\nReverie main moved again after the pinned-tree measurement. A separate compatibility population used the same Hermit source commit `76b078dd5e64e5a3bdfec8d6d187db0f1094dca5` with exact Reverie main `d3cd29e2fef334108a4e99739a6a38a744628702`, the same kernel and capabilities, and a temporary checkout whose only tracked changes were the ten Reverie pin/lock files. These results are not mixed with the clean pinned-tree population:\n\n- SaBRe-only Hermit build passed.\n- pthread-lifecycle: 20 matched of 20.\n- lock-granularity: 20 matched terminal comparisons; 1 additional requested run timed out before any comparison and returned `no_result`.\n- ppoll-simulation: 20 matched of 20.\n- startup-TLS control: 5 divergences in 5 terminal comparisons; 1 additional requested run timed out before any comparison and returned `no_result`.\n- Event content remained stable in the sampled logs: pthread-lifecycle has the final ordinary child exit, then the main-thread `FutexWait`, then futex syscall 26 returning `Ok(0)` in both executions; ppoll-simulation has ppoll syscall 22 followed by stdout `fstat` as syscall 23 in both executions.\n\nNo full validate was run.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\nTrigger 4: this is a core DetCore scheduling change affecting when sibling threads may run across a thread-exit boundary.\n","headRefName":"hermit-125/pthread-exit-join","headRefOid":"b3901a992c99b99a79e53adc877c8d4324b1e92e","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","color":"FFA500"}],"number":2747,"state":"OPEN","title":"Wait for physical thread exit before waking pthread joiners","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T01:08:58Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2747"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"24e15d63b2e356b878318faa968691ef3d5eb707","body":"[hermit2, dev-hermit, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nA guest can inherit the same Linux open file descriptions as Hermit and its caller. Forwarding `fcntl(F_SETFL)` could leave `O_APPEND` set on the caller's output after the guest exits; a later verification run then observes different state. This change keeps supported inherited status flags in Detcore's shared description model, preserves the caller's physical flags, and performs logical append writes after scheduler admission through a kernel append operation. It supports faithful repeated execution and record/replay of programs using redirected output.\n\nRecovered head: `46670790208e71be0b1aa0e249297c0f4b38663a`, tree `2c5ffe82932954367046ae55a43c0a94e1afa6a1`, based on main `dade02611a0988aaa2f234411c16462fdbfb5519`. The final diff has 22 paths. Original commit authors/messages and intentional empty history entries remain; earlier main prerequisite repairs have been incorporated rather than duplicated in the final product diff. This description replaces the historical seek-before-write implementation and stale source/count claims.\n\nTask: `astra-lane-drains-hermit-reverie-pr-queues-to-zero`\n\n## Summary\n\n- Inherited stdin/stdout/stderr start with their real status flags. `kcmp(KCMP_FILE)` detects descriptions already shared by those descriptors; aliases keep one logical flag word. A comparison error retains exact descriptors/errno, rejects partial initialization and is refused before scheduler startup, including both eager and lazy DBT exports. It is not silently treated as proof of different descriptions.\n- `F_SETFL` models `O_APPEND`. Only `O_NONBLOCK` is forwarded, with the logical and physical state updated together. The caller's other physical bits remain intact. Unsupported changes to `O_ASYNC`, `O_DIRECT` and `O_NOATIME`, and clearing caller-supplied physical append, retain the existing explicit `EOPNOTSUPP` refusal.\n- Nonstdio `F_GETFL` again hides scheduler-forced physical `O_NONBLOCK` while exposing the guest's logical state. The recovery preserves negative-offset rejection for `pwrite64` and `pwritev`.\n- For inherited regular-file append, all admitted write paths read the shared logical flags after resource admission. Scalar writes use a two-entry scratch iovec and the required oversized-range guard; vectors retain their original vector shape. Converted `pwritev2(RWF_APPEND)` uses offset -1 for ordinary write/writev and preserves explicit positional-write offsets. `RWF_NOAPPEND`, zero writes, partial completion and fatal Tool errors retain their distinct rules. Scratch/preparation failure still releases resources. Pipes, sockets and existing captured-output behavior keep their original paths.\n- Each recovered C fixture has a caller-owned temporary directory, lasting across all of that test's invocations. Fixture executables use Cargo's visible temporary root, preserving the original Command/Stdio aliases and assertions. Current manifest declarations and prepared selectors match measured test identities; deleted legacy graphs are not restored.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe shared description model prevents one inherited alias or verification run from leaving an unmodeled append bit on the caller. Append positioning is performed by the injected kernel write after admission; there is no pre-admission lseek or sampled EOF to become stale while waiting. Native ordered controls prove the requested admission/flag-read/release boundaries and meaningful restored defects fail those controls. The actual ptrace recording control checks the real `WriteV2` and debug syscall records and replay effects.\n\nThis changes the inherited-descriptor determinization strategy, not the verifier comparator. Existing syscall-output comparisons, test identities, deadlines and failure oracles remain. Hermit does not make arbitrary external file writers deterministic. A kernel append operation is not a claim that a multi-iteration short-write loop is atomic against every external writer.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nAccess/creation bits retain Linux's `F_SETFL` treatment. The guest model and supervisor's physical status word are deliberately distinct. `O_NONBLOCK` is the explicit physical exception needed by the current scheduler. Unsupported inherited flag transitions remain intentional, visible refusals rather than successful operations with fictitious behavior.\n\nThe scalar/vector translation preserves the measured Linux errno, content, file size, physical flags and OFD position, including invalid/negative offsets, huge buffers, zero writes and partial results. The second zero-length scalar iovec avoids Linux's one-iovec optimization changing scalar access-range precedence. The native reference campaign qualifies the measured x86-64 regular-file ABI; it does not establish every filesystem, architecture or backend.\n\nBroader operations that can modify the inherited backing object (`ftruncate`, `fchmod`, record locks) remain outside this focused flag repair. No universal stdio isolation claim is made. `kcmp` denial is now an explicit startup failure; host support is not assumed and no unchecked alternative comparison is used.\n\n## Relationship to gVisor and backend limits\n\ngVisor separates sandbox application file descriptions from the runtime's descriptions. This change uses Detcore's existing shared open-file-description model and syscall mediation; it introduces no second determinism engine.\n\nThe current Reverie pin is `8c8c0a57649c9ffbf8a7a14291a64320f64b935f`. The new actual guest qualification is ptrace. KVM remains dependent on the positioned-vector and decoder work in https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/538; its review findings and unexecuted mixed iovec-import-order case remain explicit. Existing KVM test identities/admitted operation scopes are retained, but no new KVM pass is claimed here. Native DBT export/startup controls are evidence of those paths, not DBT append guest qualification. No DBT, SaBRe or KVM append equivalence is inferred from ptrace or native success.\n\n## Validation\n\nAll numbers below retain their actual source and executable attribution. Historical native qualification is tied to its recorded source snapshots and preserved commit/blob mappings; it is not relabeled as a fresh run at this head.\n\n| Population | Actual result | Time |\n| --- | --- | ---: |\n| Alias/startup native qualification | Ten unique Rust tests, including eight new actual-path controls; all nine build/list/lint stages passed | 55.732 s total |\n| Append native handler qualification | Five new parent controls plus one nested child with 84 Linux-reference/actual-handler pairs; three existing error controls preserved | 52.899 s stages |\n| Five restored append defects | Every mutant failed the exact substantive assertion with Cargo 101; no setup/timeout/cap failure credited | 36.076 s native stages |\n| Restored positive controls | Five append parents, one child with all 84 pairs, and three original error controls passed | 7.548 s stages |\n| Original CLI at recovered head | **FAILED: 87 executed, 85 passed, one assertion failure and one timeout, 44 filtered**, all attempt 1 | 150.669 s Nextest; 276.346 s dispatch |\n| Independent full unit selection at recovered head | **FAILED: 533 executed, 529 passed, two assertion failures and two timeouts, zero filtered**, all attempt 1 | 138.436 s Nextest; 201.832 s dispatch |\n\nAll nine new stdio CLI cases passed through the original descriptor objects and assertions. The actual `inherited_append_record_replay_preserves_recorded_offsets_and_ofd` unit test passed in 2.409 seconds: native reference, nonverify ptrace record and autopilot replay returned guest exit 37; the real event reader required six `WriteV2` events and exact converted syscall arguments, recorded offsets, file bytes, physical flags and shared OFD position. It ran with info logs and strict recording. Its temporary event/log directory was removed by subsequent official run-state cleanup; the raw PASS/count entry, exact executed source/binary and runtime binding are retained, but no surviving external copy of that event archive is claimed.\n\nThe six failed test bodies are unchanged from main. This is a source comparison, **not a claim that baseline main was run and reproduced them**. Current diagnostics are:\n\n- CLI `record_classifies_a_gdbserver_replay_stage_container_child_failure`: the original 57.003-second Nextest timeout; cause remains under investigation.\n- CLI `sigint_instakill_reports_a_signal_death_not_a_policy_refusal`: unchanged guest-discovery assertion after 30.042 seconds. The independently landed main fixture incorrectly supplied unsupported `--argv0=cat`; the supported bare-`cat` repair passed the original targeted SIGINT test separately in 0.607 seconds and landed on main as `0838ee98a712b2c31ebead7594f9e0cec15c38d7`. This feature head retains the original failed fixture and result. The buffered parser diagnostic was not captured by this failed campaign.\n- Unit `dbt_public_dispatch_runs_echo_through_detcore` and `dbt_public_status_dispatch_runs_true_through_detcore`: original 57.003-second timeouts; the latter retains the actual `Unable to load client library: libc.so.6` diagnostic.\n- Unit `non_e9patch_validation_preserves_parent_component_paths`: `/usr/bin/../bin/echo` is absent in the current image.\n- Unit `requested_logs_survive_log_comparison_io_error`: its explicit mode-000 unreadability precondition failed in the current container.\n\nNo case was skipped, quarantined, retried or weakened to turn those populations green. The earlier CLI result (72 passed / 15 failed), compiler/observer failures and main manifest-gate failures remain separate. The last manifest gate remains **UNQUALIFIED**, with null node/driver statuses and post-rescue -9. The independently authorized compiler and product-only campaigns did not manufacture a gate pass or universal validation receipt.\n\nCurrent image: `localhost/hermit-hermetic-validate@sha256:882414fd2b1eb9c1bdc6924ab1cc8b508151e678ea5e07129bd891bb33bb2b52`. Normal source-bound compiler/bundle and host-script producers were used, with genuine prepared-reader checks. All 1,156 bound runtime inputs (4,588,959,581 bytes), source/index and historical inputs were retained before mutation. CLI/unit payload and conmon PIDs were checked in the exact node cgroup before release, with unchanged CPU/memory/swap/file/stream limits; both campaigns closed captures below cap and removed their exact processes/cgroups before rescue. Full cleanup and source/artifact readbacks passed. These are bounded local qualification results, not official receipt or whole-project success.\n\nRetained evidence is indexed in `/tmp/astra-reverie-2694-recovery/stdio-publication-plan/evidence-map.json`, with full source/history proof in `source-proof.json`. Current CLI report: `corrected-product-runtime-result/report.md`; independent unit report: `independent-unit-runtime-result-v2/report.md`; exact original-source/binary maps and immutable raw manifests are adjacent.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\nTrigger 3 applies: inherited status flags and append behavior use a changed determinization strategy. Review the alias-error startup boundary, logical/physical nonblocking distinction, post-admission flag read, Linux scalar/vector error ordering, scratch lifetime/resource release, and actual recorder/replayer integration. The code uses already admitted write-family syscalls; this Hermit patch does not change the Reverie Tool/Guest/Backend API. The pending Reverie dependency has its own required review.\n\nThe recovery author does not supply independent approval. Historical labels and approvals refer to earlier heads. Fresh non-author Codex-family and Claude-family exact-head review is still required, and the existing review-history ambiguity must be explicitly resolved by the responsible coordinator/reviewer before landing. Speculative qualification does not waive those source-review obligations.\n","headRefName":"fix/kvm-verify-stderr-oappend-leak","headRefOid":"46670790208e71be0b1aa0e249297c0f4b38663a","isDraft":false,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","color":"FFA500"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO4w","name":"adversarial-review-codex1","description":"Codex adversarial review round 1 completed","color":"5319e7"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO-g","name":"adversarial-review-claude1","description":"Adversarial review pass, claude lane (round 1)","color":"1D76DB"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuqGNPA","name":"adversarial-review-codex2","description":"Codex adversarial review round 2 conducted","color":"BFD4F2"}],"number":2694,"state":"OPEN","title":"Preserve inherited stdio flags and append writes across run and replay","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T01:47:32Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2694"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"d252f99acb2d790cc2f6ad2a2ed8c1c85d9ef7f7","body":"[hermit2, hermit-901, unresolved, devbig030, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nThis draft consolidates the surviving OSS Buck2 work into one Hermit pull request. The Hermit branch carries the BUCK files, OSS shim, pinned bootstrap, regeneration tooling, documentation, and gitlink to the revived Reverie branch at `868d46cf1d34d042da87acb6ec8a5738198041db`.\n\nThis is progress toward a reproducible alternative build, not a successful Hermit build. `//hermit-cli:hermit` does not link, and the shared action-cache benefit across worktrees has never been measured.\n\n## What works\n\n- Buck2 is pinned by the exact upstream DotSlash descriptor for release `2026-08-01`.\n- Reindeer is pinned at `e3d72748131d3a70378055f091e0647c1edad85e` and built with `nightly-2026-05-22`.\n- Dependency regeneration was run twice from absent outputs: 297 Hermit crates and 247 Reverie crates. The two generated outputs matched and both tracked lockfiles remained unchanged.\n- The build compiler used by the recorded validation is pinned to `nightly-2026-07-12`.\n- `reverie//:reverie-ptrace` builds with the pinned public Buck2 release; the current-head record is exit 0 in 19.5 seconds.\n- Hermit and Reverie now use one third-party Buck cell in the composed build, so the duplicate `serde_core-1.0.229` trait mismatch is gone.\n\n## What does not work\n\n`//hermit-cli:hermit` does not link. An earlier checkpoint progressed from the duplicate-serde failure into `detcore` and exposed approximately six stale Cargo-versus-BUCK edges across three packages. The current head fixes the newly visible undeclared `hex` and `sha2` edges and proceeds farther, but stops in `hermit-cli:libhermit`: five existing targets are missing dependency edges (`detcore_model`, `hermit_resources`, `addr2line`, `nix`, and `object`), and Buck targets do not yet exist for `reverie_kvm`, `reverie_liteinst`, or `reverie_rpc_transport`. `hermit-resources` also has no BUCK file.\n\nThe complete third-party target pattern remains diagnostic rather than green: it is red on optional `reverie-dbt-0.2` and `reverie-sabre-0.2` build-script environment plus the historical Windows-only `winapi-0.3-build-script-run` analysis exception.\n\n## Shared action cache\n\nThe cross-worktree shared action-cache benefit is the central reason to prefer Buck2 over Cargo, but it has not been measured. That measurement needs a Buck2-built Hermit binary, which this branch does not yet produce. Bootstrap and target-compatibility results do not demonstrate the payoff.\n\n## Generated aliases\n\nReindeer emits an unversioned crate alias only when `index.is_public_target` is true, which is derived from the manifest being processed; there is no configuration knob to emit the additional aliases needed by the composed Hermit/Reverie graph.\n\n`bootstrap/regenerate-rust-deps` therefore appends 14 aliases listed in the committed `shim/third-party/rust/shared-cell-aliases.txt`. Each name is resolved against the freshly generated BUCK file instead of hard-coding a versioned target. A dependency version change continues to resolve to the generated target, while a vanished crate or a newly native alias is a hard error.\n\n## Owner landing criterion\n\nThe owner said this may land immediately only if it adds build files without touching the Cargo world. Commit `e95ab8c945bcddf70ef60c931703c61170ab72ee` fails that criterion: it changes only `rust-toolchain.toml`, pinning `nightly` to `nightly-2026-07-12` and adding the `clippy` and `rustfmt` components.\n\nThat commit is mechanically separable: it modifies one file, and no later commit touches `rust-toolchain.toml`. It was not split here. Removing it would restore a floating compiler and fail the cold-reviewer reproducibility requirement, so whether to separate it is an owner decision.\n\n## Known review boundaries\n\n- Hermit is `26276e2b266e944bc02ff775b4ceb5cb0956019e`; the draft was opened without rebasing, as requested, and is five commits behind `rrnewton/hermit:main` at `d252f99acb2d790cc2f6ad2a2ed8c1c85d9ef7f7`.\n- The gitlink uses revived Reverie `868d46cf1d34d042da87acb6ec8a5738198041db`, while `.gitmodules` still names the older `buck2` branch for `--remote` updates. Ordinary recursive checkout uses the exact gitlink; `git submodule update --remote reverie` would not.\n- Hermit Cargo manifests and lockfiles still pin Reverie main `f2e9839e5316e31c377089ae7f789515ba9d2550`; the Buck source cell uses `868d46cf`. The PR does not claim those two build paths use identical Reverie source.\n- Public DotSlash 0.5.9 was used for the recorded pinned-Buck2 validation. This host internal `/usr/bin/dotslash` rejects the public descriptor with `missing field scheme`; `docs/BUCK2_OSS.md` records the public-reviewer command and the exact divergence.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe regeneration check compares two independently generated BUCK outputs and rejects lockfile changes. No Hermit execution assurance level is claimed because this branch does not produce a Hermit binary.\n\n## Validation\n\nRecorded on the branch at the named commits, using public DotSlash 0.5.9, Buck2 release `2026-08-01`, and toolchain `nightly-2026-07-12`:\n\n- `bootstrap/regenerate-rust-deps`: exit 0; 297 Hermit crates and 247 Reverie crates; repeated generated outputs matched; both lockfiles unchanged.\n- `bootstrap/buck2 build reverie//:reverie-ptrace`: exit 0 in 19.5 seconds at current head.\n- `bootstrap/buck2 build --keep-going shim//third-party/rust/...`: expected diagnostic red described above; 14 incompatible targets skipped under the pinned release.\n- `bootstrap/buck2 build //hermit-cli:hermit`: fails at the missing first-party edges and targets listed above; no binary produced.\n\nThis consolidation action did not rerun builds or alter either pushed branch.\n\nTask: consolidate-buck2-onto-one-pr","headRefName":"revive/buck2-oss-901","headRefOid":"26276e2b266e944bc02ff775b4ceb5cb0956019e","isDraft":true,"labels":[],"number":2368,"state":"OPEN","title":"Revive the reproducible OSS Buck2 build","updatedAt":"2026-08-25T20:21:21Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2368"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"302a1a9c0fde564db0292d1ea5cabc91343e79bc","body":"[hermit2, sanity-driver, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nLand the existing M2 implementation range that makes canonical verification the only verification policy, adds the record/replay path and DBT evidence handling, updates the compatibility and scorecard consumers, and carries the corresponding tests and documentation. The scheduler and signal commits resolve the measured shutdown and fork/SIGKILL/waitpid INFO-order failures that remained after re-running the three bounded reproductions on the rescued branch. The final gate commits assign the three rescued record/replay C fixtures to their actual Cargo integration-test owner and keep every pressure self-test plan inside one checked-scorecard boundary.\n\nBefore this range, M2 work existed in local commits and an uncommitted checkout and could not be reviewed or landed as one durable artifact. After it, the complete nine-commit range is published, the inherited dirty state is preserved in `79cc84087904bf7069cef9c585a2eac041341d40`, and the two reproduced failures have focused fixes and durable regression coverage. The Node wait4 red did not reproduce on the rescued branch or the final implementation, so this PR makes no Node-specific change.\n\nTask: sanity-driver-commit-hermit-then-drive-m2\n\n## Determinism\n\nCanonical verification compares exit status, stdout, stderr, and nonempty INFO observations under the repository's `BitwiseInfoV1` policy. The scheduler shutdown fix emits the existing terminal diagnostic exactly once at the terminal observation rather than once per host-loop pass. For process-directed `SIGKILL`, Hermit forwards the real signal, keeps the killing thread inside Hermit until the scheduler no longer reports the target as live, and publishes lifecycle diagnostics through scheduler-controlled RPC before the guest can proceed to `waitpid`. Inbound-signal INFO is published only after the scheduler grants delivery. Therefore host callback timing cannot choose the compared INFO order or whether the parent reaches `waitpid` before target removal.\n\nThe fork/SIGKILL/waitpid regression runs five canonical ptrace strict verifications and requires `verdict: matched`, `bitwise_parity: true`, and nonzero compared INFO counts on both sides.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThe kernel still receives the real `SIGKILL`, and unsuccessful `kill` results return without entering the completion barrier. The parent reaps the kernel-produced child status and the regression requires `WIFSIGNALED` with `WTERMSIG == SIGKILL`; no synthetic child status is substituted. The M2 verification changes compare and report execution rather than altering guest-visible syscall results.\n\n## Validation\n\nExact head: `39829148def0dd3278e607e33c9885e248a605a6`\n\nBase: `rrnewton/hermit:main` at `302a1a9c0fde564db0292d1ea5cabc91343e79bc`\n\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — passed\n- `git diff --check` — passed\n- `env CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 cargo test -p hermit-detcore --lib` — 420 passed, 0 failed\n- `timeout --kill-after=10s 120s env CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 cargo test -p hermit --test signal_determinism -- --nocapture --test-threads=1` — 13 passed, 0 failed\n- `timeout --kill-after=10s 90s env CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 cargo test -p hermit --test record_replay record_rs_sched_yield -- --nocapture --test-threads=1` — 1 passed, 0 failed\n- `env CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 cargo clippy -p hermit-detcore -p hermit --all-targets -- -D warnings` — passed\n- `./ci/test_harness.sh audit-inventory` — passed with all 552 tracked test files classified\n- `./ci/compat-envelope/pressure-test.rs self-test` — passed, including exact/batch wording, scorecard and manifest refusal, generated-DAG, and result-identity brackets\n- fork/SIGKILL/waitpid: L2, ptrace backend, INFO log level, no relaxations — five exact-head strict verification iterations passed with matched JSON, bitwise parity, and nonzero INFO counts\n- Node wait4: L2, ptrace backend, INFO log level, no relaxations — direct bounded reproductions passed; the harness attempts that returned `no_result` were rejected before guest execution by host ptrace policy and are not counted as cell failures\n\nThe first full-profile validation at `3f71ddc2924888e4a97aa6be97e584f4d3c52efa` stopped at `gate.manifest` because the three rescued C fixtures had no inventory disposition. Commit `a9056179d042811df01e642ffe73a499aded1e54` records them as guest fixtures owned and exercised by `hermit-cli/tests/record_replay.rs`. The next run reached the same gate and exposed one new pressure self-test plan rechecking the scorecard plus a stale one-cell replay premise; `39829148def0dd3278e607e33c9885e248a605a6` fixes both without changing the four-compilation ceiling.\n\nThe full-profile run at exact head `39829148def0dd3278e607e33c9885e248a605a6` passed `gate.manifest`, including the inventory, pressure self-test, four-compilation bracket, and committed-DAG correspondence. It then stopped in `build.workspace`: Hermit reads `DbtRuntimeCallbacks.emit_evidence` and `.evidence_log_level`, but the pinned Reverie `c261050cfd41bec67e31bfd0cf6f56be008d0ebb` does not define those fields. The canonical receipt is `NEEDS-RERUN`; this is not a qualifying green receipt.\n\nThis is not a whole-workspace or all-backend completion claim. The rescued inherited range is large (118 changed paths against the listed base) and requires exact-head CI plus adversarial review before landing.\n\n## Cross-repository dependency\n\nThe required protected DBT evidence API is now durable in Reverie PR https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/459 at exact head `eae94035ea7313d0b0d1b129a9fbb9c2c30fa3d8`. That PR contains the six-path owner-authorized rescue that defines `emit_evidence`, `evidence_log_level`, the protected transport and collector, and its adversarial/live tests.\n\nThis Hermit PR cannot build or land until Reverie #459 is reviewed and landed on `rrnewton/reverie:main`, after which the Hermit pin must be deliberately advanced to its landed SHA. Reusing the ordinary diagnostic emitter would remove the protected evidence boundary and is not an acceptable workaround.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\n- Trigger 3: the range establishes canonical verification and protected DBT evidence behavior as a determinization strategy.\n- Trigger 4: the final fixes change core DetCore scheduler-controlled event ordering for terminal shutdown and process-directed `SIGKILL`.\n\nReview the full nine-commit range, with particular attention to the rescued implementation commit `79cc84087904bf7069cef9c585a2eac041341d40`, the scheduler INFO change in `60fbfd612b57b615f152f071a6bbcac09064185e`, and the SIGKILL completion barrier in `3f71ddc2924888e4a97aa6be97e584f4d3c52efa`.\n","headRefName":"codex/m2-sanity-driver","headRefOid":"d16a0f1c12626ac7991a354b9597b135633ea25c","isDraft":true,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","name":"post-facto-human-review","description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","color":"FFA500"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACxmK-qQ","name":"category:other","description":"Unclassified; the owner will triage these","color":"CCCCCC"}],"number":2302,"state":"OPEN","title":"Land M2 canonical verification and fix remaining measured reds","updatedAt":"2026-09-13T11:20:36Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2302"},{"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","is_bot":false,"login":"rrnewton","name":"Ryan Newton"},"baseRefName":"main","baseRefOid":"1a679bf948dabc7a4b4233c921254d70d839e477","body":"[hermit2, degraded-unresolved, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nRestore ordinary DBT `--log-file` support using the file opened in the host namespace. The remaining work is to port this draft's logging intent onto current main, preserve the existing log-size policy, and carry the selected log level across fork and followed exec, including an empty environment.\n\nThe public draft is still the older implementation at `96eeb0d603875e595f36d6ce97fc77a998ea53a2`. Its historical guest-path approach and old validation claims are not current-main qualification. The current recovery uses the existing `DbtRunner::diagnostic_file` descriptor route as its starting point. It still needs an implementation that preserves the public logger's configured bound and explicit truncation marker, plus persistent ordinary log-level configuration.\n\nTask: `astra-hermit-drafts`, within `astra-lane-drains-hermit-reverie-pr-queues-to-zero`.\n\n## Determinism\n\nPreserve the complete selected INFO stream and existing strict comparison policy. The original guest-only INFO filtering must not be carried into the recovery. The intended change routes diagnostics without changing scheduler decisions, guest syscall results, wait ownership or the existing process-lifetime policy.\n\nOrdinary public logging has the existing diagnostic write-error and truncation behavior. It does not promise a completed evidence artifact after every descendant exits. S7's stronger evidence settlement requirements remain separate, and its ordinary DBT evidence refusal remains in force.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nPass the host-opened file descriptor through the existing diagnostic route rather than reopening a pathname in the guest. Preserve stdout/stderr, root wait status, terminal handling, PGID/SID, ordinary setsid/setpgid behavior and current workdir staging. Ordinary log routing must not create an evidence session, force a new process group, kill late children or wait for every writer solely to finalize the log.\n\n## Validation\n\nCurrent recovery analysis is read-only. It is bound to Hermit main `21ea8e42ad1ed478cffe9d36338e484939ee9cef` and its Reverie pin `8c8c0a57649c9ffbf8a7a14291a64320f64b935f`; no new runtime qualification is claimed for this draft.\n\nThe eventual focused checks must cover host-path replacement after open; fork and empty-environment exec at the selected log levels; default, unlimited, malformed and small log-size settings; explicit truncation; original root status and stream separation; and ordinary process/session/TTY behavior. A late-child control must preserve ordinary lifetime and routing without pretending that root completion settles the whole log.\n\nThe source findings and correction of an earlier overbroad S7 prerequisite are recorded in the TaskGraph notes. The report is `/tmp/astra-reverie-1689-log-route-20260914/scope-clarification/report.md`, SHA256 `ca8787160942ae6c7fe11c7be182723d30ee4287013a3f2585bfb7b3f4cf7d63`. The draft remains open for the implementation and its meaningful validation.\n","headRefName":"dbi-honour-log-file","headRefOid":"96eeb0d603875e595f36d6ce97fc77a998ea53a2","isDraft":true,"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACu2h1Mw","name":"codex-coord","description":"Owned or shepherded by codex-coord/hermit-coord","color":"1D76DB"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACvGBy_g","name":"mechanism:dbi-log-file-routing","description":"DBI log-file CLI routing into backend configuration","color":"5319e7"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACvL_BZw","name":"codex-coord-030","description":"Claimed or shepherded by codex-coord-030 on devbig030","color":"1D76DB"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACvL_fiQ","name":"claude-coord","description":"Owned or shepherded by the claude-coord autonomous team","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACvL_fnQ","name":"claude-coord-176","description":"Owned by claude-coord-176 (this coordinator instance)","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACxmK-Zg","name":"category:backends","description":"Changes for one of the non-ptrace Reverie backends; must also carry a backend: label","color":"1D76DB"},{"id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACxmK-zw","name":"backend:dbt","description":"DBT backend (CLI --backend dbt; crates reverie-dbt, detcore-dbt; DynamoRIO)","color":"5319E7"}],"number":1689,"state":"OPEN","title":"Honor DBT host log files across fork and exec","updatedAt":"2026-09-16T02:00:09Z","url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/1689"}]
